---
name: docs-humanize
description: Rewrite an AI-generated or bloated document so a human can actually read it — a Notion page, a markdown file, a Linear ticket, or a set of tickets. Use when the user says a doc is a wall of text, reads like AI slop, is full of jargon, needs tightening, needs to be made readable/scannable/useful, or when a generated spec, ticket, RFC, status update or runbook needs to be turned into something a person can act on. Five adversarial personas debate the shape, a composing editor writes it, and an auditor proves nothing real was lost and nothing new was invented.
---

# Humanize — Make an AI-Written Doc Readable by a Human

Take a document that was generated rather than written — a Notion page, a markdown file, a Linear ticket, or a set of them — and turn it into something a person reads once and acts on. Five readers with incompatible demands fight over it: an archivist who will not let a real fact disappear or a fake one appear, a skimmer who wants two thirds of it gone, a newcomer who cannot define half the nouns, a hawk who can smell generated prose at a glance, and a doer who has to start work on Monday from whatever survives. A structural editor referees and owns the shape; a composing editor writes against it; an auditor proves every load-bearing fact made it through and nothing was invented on the way.

The sibling skill `changes-description` debates a merge request description against a diff. This one has no diff. The source text is simultaneously the ground truth and the thing on trial, so the swarm's first act is to build a **fact ledger** — a numbered inventory of everything load-bearing in the source — and everything downstream is measured against it. Without that ledger, "nothing was lost" is a feeling. With it, the auditor reports *ledger item 14 is gone* and the run either restores it or says so out loud.

**Parameter:** `$ARGUMENTS` — a target, plus optional flags.

| Argument | Effect |
|---|---|
| A Notion URL | `notion.so/…`, `notion.com/…`, `*.notion.site/…` — fetched via the Notion MCP. |
| A Linear issue key | `PLAT-1180`. Several, space-separated, are processed as a set. |
| A Linear document URL | Fetched via `get_document`. |
| A file path | Any `.md`, `.mdx`, `.txt`, or plain text file in the checkout. |
| Any other URL | Fetched via `WebFetch`. |
| *(none)* | Look for a document pasted or produced earlier in this conversation; if there is none, ask what to humanize and stop. |
| `--children` | With a Linear parent key, include every child issue in the set. |
| `--as <kind>` | Force the shape preset: `ticket`, `design`, `status`, `runbook`, `reference`, `decision`. Otherwise the panel picks. |
| `--rounds N` | Cap the judging at N rounds (default `2`, max `3`). |
| `--quick` | One judging round plus a single repair pass. The repair is not re-judged, so the run reports non-unanimous. |
| `--no-write` | Print the result and skip the write-back offer entirely. |

Parse the flags out of `$ARGUMENTS` **first**; whatever remains is the target (or targets). A flag fed to the Notion fetcher as a page id produces a confusing failure three steps later.

This skill uses the **Workflow** tool. Invoking it is an explicit opt-in to multi-agent orchestration — proceed without asking for further permission. One document is one run: 21 agents at the default two rounds, 16 under `--quick`, 27 at `--rounds 3` — each plus one more if the audit finds something to repair. A set of N documents is N runs, launched one after another, plus a consistency pass you do yourself.

## Who reads this

Write for someone who **did not write this document, was not in the meeting, and has about ninety seconds before they decide whether to keep reading.** They are not stupid — they are uninformed and busy. They should finish knowing what this document is for, what is actually true, what is still unknown, and what they are supposed to do next.

That reader is why this is a fight rather than a cleanup pass. The four things they need — that it is faithful, that it is short, that it is comprehensible, and that a human thought about it — trade against each other, and a single rewriting pass quietly sells three of them to buy the fourth. The most common sale is faithfulness: a model asked to "make this readable" produces something beautiful and subtly different, and the difference is undetectable because the original is now gone.

Two failure modes matter more here than in any sibling skill:

- **Invention.** A rewrite that adds a plausible rationale, a round number, a confident claim where the source hedged, or a next step nobody agreed to, is worse than the wall of text it replaced. The wall was merely unreadable. The rewrite is wrong and readable, which means it gets believed and forwarded.
- **False certainty.** Generated documents assert. Real documents have holes. If the source does not say who owns something, the output does not guess — it says nobody is named. A document that admits three unknowns is more useful than one that smooths them over, and the swarm is instructed to treat a missing Unknowns section as a defect whenever the ledger recorded an open question.

## Step 1: Resolve the Target and Build the Packet

**Subagents cannot see this conversation.** They get exactly what the workflow prompt carries. Everything the swarm needs goes into `args` here.

### 1a. Fetch the source

Load tools via `ToolSearch` as needed — the resolution ladder is the same one `plan-subtasks` uses:

1. **Notion URL** → `select:mcp__claude_ai_Notion__notion-fetch`, passing the URL as `id`. If the fetch fails, retry once, then try `notion-search` on the page title. Also fetch comments with `notion-get-comments` — late corrections live in comments and contradict the body more often than not, and a comment that contradicts the body is a **conflict for the ledger**, never something to silently resolve.
2. **Linear issue key** → `select:mcp__claude_ai_Linear__get_issue` with `includeRelations: true`, plus `list_comments`. With `--children`, `list_issues` with `parentId` set to the parent.
3. **Linear document** → `select:mcp__claude_ai_Linear__get_document`.
4. **File path** → `Read` it. Record whether it is tracked in git (`git ls-files --error-unmatch <path>`), because that decides whether a write-back is recoverable.
5. **Other URL** → `select:WebFetch`.

If the MCP server for the target is unauthorized, **say so plainly and stop.** This skill has nothing to work from without the source, and a run against a half-fetched page produces a confident rewrite of the part that loaded.

> **Never launch with a stand-in `source`.** The script rejects an empty string and a few obvious placeholder shapes, but `"source": "the doc content goes here"` will sail through and burn a 23-agent run rewriting that sentence. Paste the real text into `args` first, before you write any of the digest around it.

Record for the packet: the exact title, the URL, the author if the tool reports one, whether the current user is that author, and the last-edited timestamp. The author matters at write-back time — overwriting someone else's page is a different act from tidying your own draft.

**Size check.** Over roughly 8000 words, do not run the whole document. Tell the user which sections you propose to take, and run them one at a time — a swarm handed 20,000 words produces a summary, which is a different deliverable and not what was asked for.

### 1b. Write the `purpose` digest

This is the part only you can do, and it is what keeps the rewrite from being a prettier version of the same confusion. Two or three plain-prose paragraphs:

- **What this document is for, and who acts on it.** A design doc read by two reviewers, a ticket picked up by one engineer, a status update skimmed by a manager, a runbook opened at 3am. The shape follows from this and from nothing else.
- **What the user said about it** — what they think is wrong with it, which parts they care about, anything they have already decided is out of scope.
- **Anything you know that the document does not say**: the real decision behind a vague paragraph, the person who actually owns a thing, the ticket this belongs to. Mark each one explicitly as *external context, not in the source* — the archivist needs to distinguish a fact you supplied from a fact the swarm hallucinated, and it cannot do that if they arrive in the same prose.
- **What you are unsure about.** A stated gap makes the auditor suspicious in the right place. An unstated gap gets confidently filled.

If the conversation is thin, say so rather than padding. Never invent rationale to make the packet look complete — the swarm will polish an invention into something indistinguishable from a fact.

### 1c. Build the glossary (sets only)

When the target is several documents, read all of them first and extract a shared glossary: every internal noun, acronym, service name, and status word, each with the one plain-language gloss that will be used in **every** document of the set. Pass it into every run as `glossary`. Without it, ticket one says "resource set", ticket four says "grant bundle", and the reader concludes they are different things.

## Step 2: Run the Swarm

Call the **Workflow** tool with the script below, passing `args` as a **real JSON object, never a JSON-encoded string**:

```json
{ "source": "<the full source text, verbatim>",
  "sourceMeta": { "kind": "notion|linear|markdown|url", "title": "…", "url": "…", "author": "…", "isOwn": true, "edited": "…" },
  "purpose": "<the Step 1b digest>",
  "glossary": "<the Step 1c glossary, or empty>",
  "preset": "<ticket|design|status|runbook|reference|decision, or empty to let the panel choose>",
  "rounds": 2 }
```

`rounds` is `2` by default, **`1` under `--quick`**, or the number given to `--rounds` (the script clamps it to 1–3 regardless). `--quick` is nothing but that value: if you leave `rounds` at 2, the swarm runs a full two-round debate and the user is told it was quick.

A JSON-encoded string makes every `args.x` lookup `undefined`; the script hard-throws rather than letting five personas argue about nothing.

**Always pass the script inline via `script`.** Never re-run this skill's workflow with `scriptPath` or `resumeFromRunId` — those replay a frozen snapshot from the run they were taken in, silently discarding any fix made to this file since. The one sanctioned exception is `/workflow-resume`, which pairs `resumeFromRunId` with the script re-read from **this** file.

````js
export const meta = {
  name: 'docs-humanize',
  description: 'Five adversarial readers rebuild an AI-written doc; an auditor proves nothing was lost or invented',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Inventory' },
    { title: 'Shape' },
    { title: 'Compose' },
    { title: 'Judge' },
    { title: 'Audit' },
  ],
}

// args = { source, sourceMeta, purpose, glossary, preset, rounds }
const A = typeof args === 'string' ? JSON.parse(args) : (args || {})
for (const k of ['source', 'purpose']) {
  if (typeof A[k] !== 'string' || !A[k].trim()) {
    throw new Error(
      `docs-humanize: args.${k} is missing or empty (got ${typeof A[k]}). ` +
      `Refusing to run — the personas would rewrite a document that does not exist. ` +
      `Pass args as a JSON OBJECT, not a JSON-encoded string.`
    )
  }
}

// A short source that announces itself as a stand-in is the one failure the empty
// check misses, and it costs a full run to discover.
if (/^[\s\S]{0,400}$/.test(A.source) && /\b(placeholder|goes here|lorem ipsum|your text here)\b/i.test(A.source)) {
  throw new Error('docs-humanize: args.source looks like a placeholder, not a document. Paste the real text.')
}

const ROUNDS = Math.min(Math.max(Number(A.rounds) || 2, 1), 3)
const META = A.sourceMeta || {}

const countWords = (s) => (s && s.trim()
  ? s.trim().split(/\s+/).filter((t) => !/^[—–*+\-:;,.#>|]+$/.test(t)).length
  : 0)

const stripCode = (s) => (s || '').replace(/```[\s\S]*?```/g, '')
const sourceWords = countWords(stripCode(A.source).replace(/^#{1,6}\s.*$/gm, ''))

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Slop is measured, not sensed. HARD patterns have no legitimate use in a
// technical document — there is always a plainer word, and their presence is a
// defect the judges must block on. SOFT patterns are ordinary English that
// generated prose over-uses ("ensure the service is running" is a real runbook
// line), so they are counted and shown to the hawk as evidence, never as a
// breach. Mixing the two makes the whole measure unusable: one false positive on
// a legitimate "robust" teaches the panel to ignore the list.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const HARD_SLOP = [
  /\bdelve[sd]?\b/gi, /\btapestry\b/gi, /\ba testament to\b/gi,
  /\bin today's (fast[- ]paced|fast[- ]moving|digital|modern|competitive|data[- ]driven|ever)\b/gi,
  /\bever-(evolving|changing|growing)\b/gi, /\bsynerg(y|ies|istic)\b/gi, /\bmyriad\b/gi,
  /\bthe realm of\b/gi, /\bnavigat(e|ing) the (complex |ever-)?landscape\b/gi,
  /\bit('s| is) (important|worth) (to note|noting)\b/gi, /\bat the end of the day\b/gi,
  /\bthat being said\b/gi, /\bplays? an? (crucial|key|vital|pivotal|integral) role\b/gi,
  /\bunlock(ing)? the (power|potential)\b/gi, /\bempower(s|ed|ing)?\b/gi,
  /\bcutting[- ]edge\b/gi, /\bseamless(ly)?\b/gi, /\bholistic(ally)?\b/gi,
  /\bparadigm shift\b/gi, /\bdeep dive into\b/gi, /\bwhen it comes to\b/gi,
  /\bkey takeaways?\b/gi, /\brest assured\b/gi,
  /\bunderscor(es|ing) (the|a|an|our|its|why|how)\b/gi, /\bvibrant\b/gi, /\bleverag(e|es|ed|ing)\b/gi,
  /\butiliz(e|es|ed|ing)\b/gi, /\bfacilitat(e|es|ed|ing)\b/gi, /\bin order to\b/gi,
  /\bgame[- ]chang(er|ing)\b/gi, /(?:^|[.!?]\s+)(overall|in conclusion|in summary)[,:]/gi,
  /\bthis (document|section) (outlines|serves to|aims to|will cover)\b/gi,
  /\bnot only\b[^.!?]{0,80}\bbut also\b/gi,
]
const SOFT_SLOP = [
  /\brobust\b/gi, /\bcomprehensive\b/gi, /\bstreamlin(e|es|ed|ing)\b/gi,
  /\bensur(e|es|ed|ing)\b/gi, /\bcrucial\b/gi, /\bfurthermore\b/gi, /\bmoreover\b/gi,
  /\badditionally\b/gi, /\bmoving forward\b/gi, /\bis designed to\b/gi,
  /\bbest practices?\b/gi, /\bactionable insights?\b/gi, /\bsignificantly\b/gi,
  /\bfoster(s|ed|ing)?\b/gi,
]

// Matched against a whitespace-flattened copy, never the raw text. A markdown
// paragraph wraps wherever the author's editor wrapped it, so a line break lands
// inside a multi-word phrase often enough that "plays a crucial role" goes
// undetected in exactly the documents this list exists to catch.
const hits = (text, pats) => {
  const flat = (text || '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
  const found = []
  for (const re of pats) {
    const m = flat.match(re)
    if (m && m.length) found.push({ phrase: m[0].toLowerCase().replace(/^[^a-z]+/, '').trim(), count: m.length })
  }
  return found
}

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Structure, measured. A generated document fails structurally far more often
// than it fails sentence by sentence: headings over two-sentence stubs, every
// bullet shaped `- **Noun**: clause`, paragraphs nobody's eye can land in. None
// of that is visible to an agent asked "is this readable?" — it answers yes.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A numbered list is a list. Treating `1.` as prose made a twelve-step runbook
// read as one 144-word paragraph and the nesting and bold-lede checks blind to
// exactly the presets built on ordered lists, so every list test below matches
// both markers. Fenced code is stripped from the word count and the paragraph
// scan for the same reason: `code` is an offered container, and measuring it as
// prose punishes a document for using it.
const LIST = /^(\s*)(?:[-*+]|\d+[.)])\s+/
const measure = (text) => {
  const t = stripCode(text || '')
  const lines = t.split('\n')
  const headings = lines.filter((l) => /^#{1,6}\s/.test(l))
  const sections = []
  let cur = null
  for (const l of lines) {
    if (/^#{1,6}\s/.test(l)) {
      if (cur) sections.push(cur)
      cur = { heading: l.replace(/^#+\s*/, '').trim(), level: (l.match(/^#+/) || ['#'])[0].length, body: [] }
    } else if (cur) cur.body.push(l)
  }
  if (cur) sections.push(cur)

  const listLines = lines.filter((l) => LIST.test(l))
  const boldLede = listLines.filter((l) => /^\s*(?:[-*+]|\d+[.)])\s+\*\*[^*]+\*\*\s*[:—-]/.test(l))
  // Nesting depth is relative to the shallowest indent actually used, because a
  // formatter that indents by four spaces otherwise reports one level as three.
  const indents = listLines.map((l) => l.match(LIST)[1].replace(/\t/g, '    ').length)
  const unit = indents.filter((i) => i > 0).sort((a, b) => a - b)[0] || 2
  const maxDepth = indents.length ? Math.max(...indents.map((i) => Math.floor(i / unit) + 1)) : 0

  const paragraphs = t.split(/\n\s*\n/).filter((p) => p.trim() && !/^\s*(?:[-*+#>|]|\d+[.)]\s)/.test(p.trim()))
  const longestPara = paragraphs.reduce((m, p) => Math.max(m, countWords(p)), 0)

  const prose = t.replace(/^#{1,6}\s.*$/gm, '').replace(/^\s*\|.*$/gm, '')
  const sentences = prose.split(/[.!?]+(?:\s+|$)/).map((s) => s.trim()).filter((s) => countWords(s) > 2)
  const lens = sentences.map(countWords).sort((a, b) => a - b)
  const p90 = lens.length ? lens[Math.min(lens.length - 1, Math.floor(lens.length * 0.9))] : 0

  const words = countWords(t.replace(/^#{1,6}\s.*$/gm, ''))
  // A heading immediately followed by a deeper heading is a parent, not a stub —
  // the `reference` preset asks for exactly that one-line orientation.
  const thin = sections.filter((sec, i) => {
    const next = sections[i + 1]
    if (next && next.level > sec.level) return false
    if (i === 0 && sec.level === 1) return false
    const b = sec.body.join('\n')
    const bodyLines = b.split('\n').filter((l) => l.trim())
    if (bodyLines.length && bodyLines.every((l) => /^\s*`[^`]+`/.test(l))) return false
    return countWords(b) > 0 && countWords(b) < 12 && !/^\s*(?:[-*+|]|\d+[.)]\s)/m.test(b)
  }).map((sec) => sec.heading)

  return {
    words,
    ratio: sourceWords ? Number((words / sourceWords).toFixed(2)) : 0,
    headings: headings.length,
    sections: sections.length,
    thinSections: thin,
    bullets: listLines.length,
    boldLedeRatio: listLines.length ? Number((boldLede.length / listLines.length).toFixed(2)) : 0,
    maxDepth,
    longestParagraph: longestPara,
    longestSentence: lens.length ? lens[lens.length - 1] : 0,
    p90Sentence: p90,
    sentenceCount: lens.length,
    hardSlop: hits(t, HARD_SLOP),
    softSlop: hits(t, SOFT_SLOP),
    emoji: (t.match(/[\u{1F300}-\u{1FAFF}\u{2700}-\u{27BF}]/gu) || []).length,
  }
}

const SOURCE_BLOCK = `SOURCE DOCUMENT — title: ${META.title || '(untitled)'}${META.url ? `, url: ${META.url}` : ''}${META.author ? `, author: ${META.author}` : ''}
It is ${sourceWords} words. This text is the ONLY ground truth you have; nothing outside it
and the purpose digest below may enter the rewrite.

<<<SOURCE
${A.source}
SOURCE>>>`

const PURPOSE_BLOCK = `PURPOSE DIGEST (what this document is for, who acts on it, and what the operator knows that the source does not say — anything marked as external context is a real fact you may use, and the ONLY material permitted from outside the source):
${A.purpose}${A.glossary ? `\n\nSHARED GLOSSARY — use these exact terms and glosses, this document is part of a set:\n${A.glossary}` : ''}`

const LEDGER_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    items: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          id: { type: 'string' },
          text: { type: 'string' },
          kind: { type: 'string', enum: ['claim', 'number', 'name', 'decision', 'constraint', 'owner', 'date', 'action', 'open-question', 'link'] },
          loadBearing: { type: 'boolean' },
          why: { type: 'string' },
        },
        required: ['id', 'text', 'kind', 'loadBearing'],
        additionalProperties: false,
      },
    },
    conflicts: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: { a: { type: 'string' }, b: { type: 'string' }, note: { type: 'string' } },
        required: ['a', 'b'],
        additionalProperties: false,
      },
    },
    purposeRead: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['items', 'purposeRead'],
  additionalProperties: false,
}

const AUDIT_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    provenance: { type: 'string', enum: ['generated', 'mixed', 'human'] },
    evidence: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    density: { type: 'string', enum: ['padded', 'normal', 'dense'] },
    worstFailure: { type: 'string', enum: ['structure', 'jargon', 'padding', 'false-certainty', 'no-point'] },
    notes: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['provenance', 'density', 'worstFailure', 'evidence'],
  additionalProperties: false,
}

const SHAPE_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    preset: { type: 'string' },
    spine: { type: 'string' },
    outline: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          heading: { type: 'string' },
          container: { type: 'string', enum: ['prose', 'bullets', 'table', 'checklist', 'code'] },
          carries: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
          why: { type: 'string' },
        },
        required: ['heading', 'container', 'carries'],
        additionalProperties: false,
      },
    },
    cut: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, reason: { type: 'string' } },
        required: ['id', 'reason'],
        additionalProperties: false,
      },
    },
    rationale: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['spine', 'outline', 'rationale'],
  additionalProperties: false,
}

const DOC_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    doc: { type: 'string' },
    spine: { type: 'string' },
    rationale: { type: 'string' },
    unresolved: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
  },
  required: ['doc', 'spine', 'rationale'],
  additionalProperties: false,
}

const JUDGE_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    verdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['accept', 'revise'] },
    blocking: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          quote: { type: 'string' },
          problem: { type: 'string' },
          fix: { type: 'string' },
        },
        required: ['quote', 'problem', 'fix'],
        additionalProperties: false,
      },
    },
    note: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['verdict', 'blocking'],
  additionalProperties: false,
}

const COVERAGE_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    missing: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, text: { type: 'string' }, whyItMatters: { type: 'string' } },
        required: ['id', 'text', 'whyItMatters'],
        additionalProperties: false,
      },
    },
    invented: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: { quote: { type: 'string' }, problem: { type: 'string' }, correction: { type: 'string' } },
        required: ['quote', 'problem', 'correction'],
        additionalProperties: false,
      },
    },
    distorted: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, quote: { type: 'string' }, problem: { type: 'string' }, correction: { type: 'string' } },
        required: ['quote', 'problem', 'correction'],
        additionalProperties: false,
      },
    },
    verdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['faithful', 'repair'] },
  },
  required: ['missing', 'invented', 'verdict'],
  additionalProperties: false,
}

// ── Phase 1: the ledger and the diagnosis ───────────────────────────────────
// These two run together and neither depends on the other. The ledger is the
// substrate every later phase is measured against; the diagnosis decides how
// hard this document should be compressed, which is why the compression band
// is computed AFTER this phase rather than guessed from word count alone.
phase('Inventory')
const [ledger, audit] = await parallel([
  () => agent(
    `You are an archivist building a fact ledger. You are not rewriting anything and you have no opinion about style.

Read the source document below and extract EVERY load-bearing item, one per entry. A load-bearing item is anything whose loss would change what a reader believes or does: a factual claim, a number, a named system or person or team, a decision that was made, a constraint, an owner, a date or deadline, an action someone must take, an open question, or a link.

Rules that make this ledger usable downstream:
- Quote or tightly paraphrase the item in \`text\` — enough that a later agent can check whether it survived, without needing the source.
- Give every item a stable id: F1, F2, F3…
- \`loadBearing\` is false for throat-clearing, restated headings, and sentences that assert a benefit with no fact behind them. Record them anyway — a later phase needs to know they were seen and deliberately dropped, not missed.
- An item the document HEDGES ("we think", "roughly", "TBD", "pending confirmation") keeps its hedge in \`text\`. Stripping it is how a maybe becomes a fact.
- Anything the source leaves genuinely unknown — an unnamed owner, an undecided approach, a number marked TBD — is an \`open-question\` item. These are the items most often lost in a rewrite, and losing them is the worst outcome available here. An open question is a DECISION NOBODY HAS MADE, not work nobody has started. "Protobuf or Parquet at rest" is one; "the migration script is not written" is not — that is an \`action\`, however far from done it is, and so is a defect with an obvious remedy. Recording unstarted work as an open question manufactures uncertainty the source does not have, and a later phase will dutifully print it beside the very bullet it came from.
- If two parts of the source contradict each other, record BOTH as items and list the pair in \`conflicts\`. Do not pick a winner; that is not your job and the author may not know yet.

In \`purposeRead\`, say in one sentence what this document is actually for, judged from its content rather than from its title.

${SOURCE_BLOCK}

${PURPOSE_BLOCK}`,
    { label: 'inventory:ledger', phase: 'Inventory', schema: LEDGER_SCHEMA }
  ),
  () => agent(
    `You are diagnosing a document before it is rewritten. Do not rewrite anything.

Decide three things about the source below and support each with evidence quoted from it.

1. \`provenance\` — was this written by a person, generated by a model, or mixed? Evidence for generated: uniform sentence rhythm, every bullet shaped the same way, benefits asserted without facts, headings over two-sentence sections, hedges with nothing behind them, symmetrical lists whose content is not symmetrical, a closing paragraph restating the opening. Evidence for human: uneven rhythm, specific numbers nobody would invent, asides, admissions of uncertainty, an opinion.
2. \`density\` — \`padded\` (most sentences carry nothing a reader would act on), \`normal\`, or \`dense\` (every sentence carries a fact; this document is hard to read for some other reason).
3. \`worstFailure\` — the single biggest reason a reader bounces off this: \`structure\` (the information is there but in no usable order), \`jargon\` (undefined nouns), \`padding\` (volume), \`false-certainty\` (asserts what it does not know), or \`no-point\` (the reader cannot tell what it is for or what to do).

Quote at least three pieces of evidence. A diagnosis with no quotes is a guess, and the whole run is calibrated on your answer.

${SOURCE_BLOCK}

${PURPOSE_BLOCK}`,
    { label: 'inventory:diagnose', phase: 'Inventory', schema: AUDIT_SCHEMA }
  ),
])

if (!ledger || !ledger.items || !ledger.items.length) {
  throw new Error('docs-humanize: the ledger agent produced no items — refusing to rewrite a document nothing is measured against.')
}

const items = ledger.items
const bearing = items.filter((i) => i.loadBearing)
const openQuestions = items.filter((i) => i.kind === 'open-question')
const provenance = (audit && audit.provenance) || 'mixed'
const density = (audit && audit.density) || 'normal'
const worstFailure = (audit && audit.worstFailure) || 'structure'

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The compression band, derived rather than fixed. A single "cut it in half"
// rule is wrong in both directions: it mangles a dense human document and lets a
// padded generated one off easy. A short source may legitimately GROW — a
// four-line ticket that hides three open questions gets a section naming them —
// so the upper bound rises as the source shrinks. The band is a band and not a
// target: landing inside it is required, landing at its floor is not a win.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const BANDS = {
  generated: { padded: [0.25, 0.5], normal: [0.35, 0.6], dense: [0.5, 0.8] },
  mixed:     { padded: [0.35, 0.6], normal: [0.5, 0.8],  dense: [0.65, 0.95] },
  human:     { padded: [0.5, 0.8],  normal: [0.7, 1.0],  dense: [0.85, 1.25] },
}
let [floor, ceil] = BANDS[provenance][density]
if (sourceWords < 250) { floor = Math.min(floor, 0.6); ceil = Math.max(ceil, 1.4) }
const LIMITS = {
  maxSentence: 32,
  p90Sentence: 24,
  maxParagraph: 110,
  maxDepth: 2,
  hardSlop: 0,
  emoji: 0,
  boldLedeRatio: 0.5,
}

const bandText = `MEASURED LIMITS (computed from the source, not estimated):
- Length: between ${Math.round(sourceWords * floor)} and ${Math.round(sourceWords * ceil)} words (source is ${sourceWords}; it reads as ${provenance}/${density}, so the band is ${floor}×–${ceil}×). The ceiling blocks; the floor does not. Landing under it is reported for a human to glance at, and is never fixed by adding words.
- No sentence over ${LIMITS.maxSentence} words, and the 90th-percentile sentence at or under ${LIMITS.p90Sentence}. Flat rhythm is the loudest generated-prose tell, so VARY length — the limit is on the long tail, not an instruction to write uniformly short sentences.
- No paragraph over ${LIMITS.maxParagraph} words.
- Bullet nesting at most ${LIMITS.maxDepth} deep.
- ZERO hard-slop phrases (the list is checked mechanically and reported to you verbatim).
- No emoji.
- At most ${LIMITS.boldLedeRatio * 100}% of bullets may use the \`- **Noun**: clause\` shape. It is not wrong; it is the house style of generated documents, and a page of them reads as machine output however true each line is.

Every number is a CEILING or a band, never a quota. A document comfortably inside its range is not missing anything by definition. Never pad to reach a floor, and never merge two real ideas to look tighter.`

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Shape presets. Free-form structure is how a rewrite ends up with eleven
// headings and no order; a preset gives the panel something to argue WITH. They
// are starting points and may be deviated from, but only out loud, in the
// editor's rationale — an unexplained deviation is how "we had no shape" gets
// relabelled as "we chose a bespoke shape".
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const PRESETS = {
  ticket: `Ticket — someone is assigned this and must start work.
  Problem (what is broken or missing, concretely) · Scope (what this ticket does and explicitly does not do) ·
  Acceptance criteria (checklist, each line independently verifiable) · Context (links, prior art, the ONE paragraph
  of background needed) · Unknowns (what is not decided, and who decides it).`,
  design: `Design doc — reviewers must be able to disagree with it.
  What we are doing and why (one short section, lead with the problem) · Constraints (what is fixed and not up for
  debate) · Approach (the chosen one, stated plainly) · Alternatives considered and why they lost (one line each,
  cut entirely if the source does not say) · Risks and open questions · What happens next, and who does it.`,
  status: `Status update — a busy reader skims it in forty seconds.
  Where it stands (one paragraph, lead with the honest verdict) · What moved since last time · What is blocked and
  on whom · What is next · Dates and numbers, stated plainly. Never a section per workstream when three of them
  have nothing to report.`,
  runbook: `Runbook — someone opens this at 3am, possibly on a phone.
  When to use this (the symptom that brings you here) · Preconditions · Steps, numbered, imperative, one action
  each, with the expected result of each · How to tell it worked · What to do when it did not · Who to escalate to.
  Prose is the enemy here; steps are the document.`,
  reference: `Reference — read by lookup, not front to back.
  A one-paragraph orientation, then the material organised for scanning: tables for anything comparable, a stable
  heading per lookup target, no narrative between entries. The reader arrived knowing what they want.`,
  decision: `Decision record — written once, read in two years by someone wondering why.
  The decision, stated in one sentence at the top · Status and date · Context that forced it · What was decided ·
  What was rejected and why · Consequences, including the bad ones. Past tense; this is a record, not a proposal.`,
}
const presetKey = (A.preset || '').trim().toLowerCase()
const presetBlock = PRESETS[presetKey]
  ? `SHAPE PRESET — the operator specified \`${presetKey}\`. Use it:\n${PRESETS[presetKey]}`
  : `SHAPE PRESETS — pick the ONE that matches what this document is for, and say which in \`preset\`:\n${Object.entries(PRESETS).map(([k, v]) => `- ${k}: ${v}`).join('\n')}\nDeviate only if none fits, and say why in \`rationale\`.`

const HOUSE = `
NON-NEGOTIABLE HOUSE RULES — no persona may argue against these:

1. NOTHING IS INVENTED. Every sentence must trace to a ledger item or to something the
   purpose digest explicitly marks as external context. No added rationale, no rounded
   number, no inferred owner, no plausible next step that nobody agreed to. A rewrite that
   is readable and slightly wrong is worse than the wall of text it replaced, because the
   wall was at least obviously unfinished.
2. UNCERTAINTY SURVIVES AS UNCERTAINTY. A hedge in the source stays a hedge. An
   open-question ledger item appears in the output — in its own named section when there
   are two or more. A document that quietly resolves what the source left open has lied in
   the most expensive way available. An open question the source ALREADY states in a
   standing header line is already in the output; restating it in the body is duplication,
   not fidelity, and a judge is right to block it. The same holds for a gap the document
   already lists as a defect or an action: naming it again as an open question is one
   sentence twice, and the restatement is the copy to cut.
3. CONFLICTS ARE SURFACED, NOT RESOLVED. Where the source contradicts itself, say so in one
   line and name both versions. You are not the person who gets to decide.
4. LEAD WITH THE POINT. The first paragraph says what this is and what it is for. No
   throat-clearing, no "this document outlines", no restating the title.
5. EVERY TERM IS EXPLAINED OR DROPPED. An acronym or internal noun is defined at first use
   in six words, or the concrete thing is named instead and the term disappears. Prefer the
   second — a gloss added to a list makes a longer list.
6. THE SUBSTITUTION TEST. Every sentence must be FALSE of some other document. A sentence
   that would survive unchanged in a different doc carries no information about this one.
   This is the single most reliable detector of text that was generated rather than thought
   about.
7. PICK THE RIGHT CONTAINER. Prose for reasoning, tables for things being compared,
   numbered steps for sequence, checklists for things to verify. Bulleting everything is
   the default of a writer who has not decided what the content is. A bullet carrying a
   subordinate clause and a parenthetical is a paragraph wearing a hat.
8. ONE IDEA PER BULLET, and the bullet opens with the thing itself, not with its
   justification.
9. NO SECTION THAT SAYS "N/A". If there is nothing to say, the heading does not exist. A
   heading over a two-sentence section is not a section.
10. NO EDITORIALIZING. No "greatly improves", "robust solution", "much cleaner". A benefit
   is stated as the fact that produces it, or not at all.
11. PRESERVE THE AUTHOR'S VOICE WHERE THERE IS ONE. If the source reads as human-written,
   you are editing, not replacing: keep their sentences where they work, keep their asides
   and opinions, and cut rather than rephrase. Free-hand rewriting is licensed only on
   prose that was generated. Flattening a real person into house style is a failure even
   when the result is more polished.
12. IDENTIFIERS ARE COPIED, NEVER PARAPHRASED. Service names, ticket keys, config keys,
   file paths, people's names, dates and numbers appear exactly as the source has them.
   Never invent a URL; a link either came from the source or does not appear.
13. FORMATTING IS QUIET. No emoji. Bold for a genuine term of art at first use, not for
   every noun. No horizontal rules between every section. Markdown, and nothing exotic.
14. LENGTH TRACKS THE CONTENT, not the length of the source and not the length of the
   conversation about it.

${bandText}
A limit breach is not a style opinion; it is a defect, and "the subject is complicated" is
not a defence. Complicated subjects are exactly when a reader needs the short version.
`

// Deliberately lopsided charters. A persona that pre-compromises is useless; the
// structural editor is the only participant allowed to be balanced.
const PERSONAS = [
  {
    key: 'archivist',
    title: 'the archivist who holds the original and trusts nobody with it',
    axis: 'fidelity — nothing real lost, nothing new invented',
    charter: `You have watched a dozen documents get "cleaned up" into something subtly
different, and nobody noticed because the original was gone. You hold the fact ledger. Your
fear is twofold and both halves are fatal: a load-bearing item that quietly vanished, and a
confident sentence nobody can trace to the source. You are NOT arguing for length — a short
faithful document is your ideal outcome. You are arguing that what remains is true and that
what was dropped was genuinely droppable.`,
    veto: `Block ONLY when: (a) a load-bearing ledger item is absent from the output — name
its id; (b) a claim appears that traces to no ledger item and to nothing the purpose digest
marked as external context — this is your most important veto and the hardest to use,
because invented sentences read exactly like real ones and usually read better; (c) a hedge
became a certainty, or an open question was silently answered; (d) a number, name,
identifier or date was changed, rounded, or paraphrased; (e) a conflict recorded in the
ledger was silently resolved in favour of one side; or (f) the source reads as human-written
and a passage with a real voice was flattened into house style for no reason but polish.
Wanting a non-load-bearing item kept is a preference, not a veto.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'skimmer',
    title: 'a busy reader with ninety seconds and a full inbox',
    axis: 'compression',
    charter: `You did not write this, you were not in the meeting, and two more documents are
waiting. You read the first paragraph and the headings; if that does not tell you what this
is and what it wants from you, you close it. You despise: restating what the next section
says, background nobody asked for, sections included out of habit, a sentence you could
delete without the reader losing anything, and any paragraph you cannot land your eye in.
Half of most generated documents is subtraction waiting to happen.`,
    veto: `Block ONLY when: (a) a sentence or section carries nothing a reader would act on
or believe differently for; (b) two parts say the same thing; (c) the MEASURED LIMITS are
breached — you are handed the real numbers, so this is arithmetic, not taste, and you must
block on every breach listed; (d) a section exists out of habit rather than because it
carries something; or (e) the document makes its reader wait: the point arrives in paragraph
three. Two things are NOT vetoes. An explanation the newcomer needs — see the tie-breaks.
And an Unknowns section: it is required by house rule when the ledger has open questions,
and your win against it is making it three lines, never zero.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'newcomer',
    title: 'a capable person who is new here and has read none of the background',
    axis: 'comprehensibility',
    charter: `You are going to be asked to act on this document. You are not stupid — you are
unfamiliar. Nobody has told you what the internal nouns mean, which of the four similarly
named services this one is, or what the project is for. Ask the dumb question out loud every
time: what IS that? why does it matter? what happens if it is wrong? If a term appears you
cannot define from this text alone, you will either nod along or spend an hour reconstructing
context, and both are failures of the document.`,
    veto: `Block ONLY when: (a) a term, acronym, internal noun or concept appears that a
competent newcomer could not define from this text alone; (b) the document assumes context
from a meeting, a ticket, or a sibling doc that is not restated here — including a bare
allusion to a limit, a cap, or a failure whose actual number lives elsewhere; (c) after
reading it you still cannot say what it is for; (d) the FIRST PARAGRAPH fails on its own; or
(e) IT DOES NOT HANG TOGETHER — you cannot say why these sections are in one document, or
the order is one you cannot detect a reason for. That last one is your most valuable veto
and the easiest to under-use: a set of sections you understand individually is still a set,
and you cannot act on it. When you block on (e), the fix is almost always ORDERING or
CUTTING, not explaining — say which. Wanting more words is never a veto.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'hawk',
    title: 'an editor who can smell generated prose from across the room',
    axis: 'thought — sentences that could only have been written about this',
    charter: `You have read four hundred of these and you can tell inside two sentences
whether a person thought about this or a model filled in a template. What you hunt is prose
that pattern-matches to "good writing" while carrying no thought: confident sentences equally
true of any other document; benefits asserted with no fact behind them; a section that
restates the one above it one abstraction level up; tricolons of adjectives; symmetrical
bullets whose content is not symmetrical; a closing paragraph summarizing what was just
said; and the flat uniform rhythm of generated text, where every sentence is the same length
and no sentence surprises you. You are NOT hunting short or plain text — a plain sentence
packed with specifics is exactly right. You are hunting text nobody thought about, because a
reader who smells it stops trusting every other sentence, including the true ones.`,
    veto: `Block ONLY when: (a) a sentence would survive unchanged in a DIFFERENT document —
apply the substitution test literally, it is your sharpest tool; (b) a benefit or quality is
claimed with no concrete fact behind it; (c) a section restates another, one abstraction
level up; (d) hard-slop phrasing appears — you are handed the mechanical count, and it is a
defect, not a preference; (e) the rhythm is uniform enough to read as machine output, or
every bullet wears the same \`**Noun**:\` costume; or (f) the SPINE itself would fit a hundred
other documents. A generic spine is the worst case, because every sentence downstream
inherits it. Plain language is NOT slop — that is the newcomer's win, and you may not block
it. Brevity is not your axis either: a short empty sentence is your target, a short specific
one is not.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'doer',
    title: 'the person who gets assigned this on Monday morning',
    axis: 'actionability',
    charter: `Somebody is going to hand you this document and expect work to come out of it.
You need to know what you are being asked to do, how you will know when it is done, what is
already decided versus still open, and who to ask when you get stuck. You have been burned
by documents that described a situation beautifully and left you unable to take a single
step. You do not want more detail — you want the few specific things that convert reading
into doing. If the source genuinely does not contain them, you want that said out loud, not
papered over: "no owner is named" is a useful sentence and "the team will coordinate" is not.`,
    veto: `Block ONLY when: (a) a reader cannot tell what they are supposed to DO after
reading — for a ticket, that means no verifiable acceptance criteria; for a decision or
design, no statement of what happens next; for a runbook, a step that is not an action;
(b) something presented as decided is not traceable to a decision in the ledger, or something
the ledger records as open is presented as settled; (c) an action exists with no owner and
the source names one; (d) a step or criterion is not independently verifiable — "improve
performance" is not a criterion, "p99 under 400ms on the staging dataset" is; or (e) the
document's open questions are buried in prose instead of being listed where someone could
pick them up. You may NOT block to demand detail the source does not contain — if it is not
there, your remedy is a one-line statement that it is unknown, never an invention. That
boundary is absolute and the archivist will block you if you cross it.`,
  },
]

const REFEREE = `
TIE-BREAK RULES (the structural editor applies these; personas may not override them):
- Archivist vs anyone: fidelity wins. Always. A fact may be moved, compressed, or plainly
  reworded, but it may not be dropped to satisfy a length, flow, or tone objection. If a
  load-bearing fact genuinely cannot be made to fit, the shape is wrong, not the fact.
- Newcomer vs skimmer: resolve by REMOVING the jargon, not by adding a gloss for it. If the
  concrete thing can simply be named, name it and delete the term. Only a genuinely
  irreducible term earns an inline explanation — and then it stays. A gloss NEVER goes
  inside a table cell: a table is the one container built for scanning down a column, and a
  parenthetical in a cell widens it and breaks that. Gloss the term in the prose that
  introduces or follows the table, or drop the term from the cell.
- Doer vs archivist: the doer never wins the right to invent. When the doer needs something
  the source does not have, the resolution is always a plain statement of the gap — "owner
  not named in the source", "no acceptance criteria were written" — placed where the doer
  would look for it.
- Hawk vs newcomer: an explanation a reader needs is never slop. A definition of something
  obvious is. If the term can be dropped by naming the concrete thing, that single edit
  satisfies both.
- Hawk vs archivist: a hedge with a real fact behind it stays — that is the source being
  honest, not the writer being vague. A hedge standing IN PLACE of a fact is slop; cut the
  sentence or state the gap plainly.
- Skimmer vs doer: a section that converts reading into doing survives even when short on
  prose value. Acceptance criteria and next steps are not padding. The skimmer's win against
  them is tightening each line, never deleting the section.
- Open questions vs everyone: they survive. The skimmer may compress them into a list; nobody
  may cut them. A document that hides what it does not know is the failure mode this skill
  exists to prevent. That protection covers REAL open questions only. One that merely
  restates a defect or an unstarted task is not an open question, and the skimmer may cut it
  outright — a section padded to justify its own heading is the opposite of admitting a gap.
- Voice vs polish: where the source reads as human-written, a passage with a real voice beats
  a smoother rewrite of the same content. Polish is not a veto condition for anyone.
- Any objection that would violate a house rule is discarded outright, however senior the
  persona raising it.
- An objection with no minimal concrete fix attached is a preference, not a block.
`

const ledgerBlock = `FACT LEDGER — ${items.length} items, ${bearing.length} load-bearing, ${openQuestions.length} open question(s).
Everything the output says must trace to one of these (or to external context named in the purpose digest).
${items.map((i) => `${i.id} [${i.kind}${i.loadBearing ? ', LOAD-BEARING' : ''}] ${i.text}`).join('\n')}${(ledger.conflicts || []).length ? `\n\nCONFLICTS IN THE SOURCE (surface, do not resolve):\n${ledger.conflicts.map((c) => `- "${c.a}" vs "${c.b}"${c.note ? ` — ${c.note}` : ''}`).join('\n')}` : ''}`

const diagnosisBlock = `DIAGNOSIS: this source reads as ${provenance}, ${density}. Its worst failure is ${worstFailure}.
${(audit && audit.evidence || []).map((e) => `- evidence: ${e}`).join('\n')}${audit && audit.notes ? `\n${audit.notes}` : ''}
What this document is actually for: ${ledger.purposeRead}`

const vetoTable = PERSONAS.map((p) => `- ${p.key} (${p.axis}): ${p.veto}`).join('\n')
const COMMON = `${ledgerBlock}\n\n${diagnosisBlock}\n\n${PURPOSE_BLOCK}\n\n${SOURCE_BLOCK}`

// ── Phase 2: the panel argues about SHAPE, not prose ────────────────────────
// Five full drafts of a long document is mostly thrown-away tokens, and the
// argument worth having is structural: what this document is for, what order
// it goes in, and what gets cut. Prose objections still happen — they happen in
// Phase 4, against text that actually exists.
phase('Shape')
const proposals = (await parallel(PERSONAS.map((p) => () =>
  agent(
    `You are ${p.title}. You are one of ${PERSONAS.length} readers with incompatible demands, and you are proposing the SHAPE of a rewritten document. You are NOT writing prose — propose structure, and nothing you propose may invent content.

Argue your corner and do not pre-compromise; an editor downstream is responsible for balance, and a proposal that already hedges gives them nothing to weigh. Your axis is ${p.axis}.

${p.charter}

${presetBlock}

Produce:
- \`spine\`: ONE sentence naming what this document is about and what it wants from its reader. This is the thing every section must serve. Your persona shapes it — the archivist's spine is the fact that must not be lost, the skimmer's is the headline, the newcomer's is the concept you must hold to follow anything, the hawk's is the one thing that could only be said about THIS document, the doer's is the work it produces.
- \`outline\`: the sections in order. For each, its \`heading\`, the right \`container\` for its content (prose / bullets / table / checklist / code), the ledger item ids it \`carries\`, and one line on \`why\` it is there and why it sits in that position.
- \`cut\`: the ledger items you propose dropping, each as an \`id\` and the \`reason\` it goes. Dropping a load-bearing item requires a real argument; dropping filler does not.
- \`rationale\`: two sentences on what your persona forced that a neutral outliner would have missed.

Every load-bearing ledger item must appear in some section's \`carries\` or in \`cut\`. An item in neither has been lost, which is the failure this whole run exists to prevent.

${HOUSE}

${COMMON}`,
    { label: `shape:${p.key}`, phase: 'Shape', schema: SHAPE_SCHEMA }
  ).then((s) => (s ? { persona: p.key, ...s } : null))
))).filter(Boolean)

if (!proposals.length) throw new Error('docs-humanize: every shape proposal failed; nothing to referee.')

const proposalBlock = proposals.map((s) =>
  `--- ${s.persona.toUpperCase()} PROPOSES (preset: ${s.preset || 'unstated'}) ---\nSpine: ${s.spine}\n${(s.outline || []).map((o, i) => `${i + 1}. ${o.heading} [${o.container}] carries ${(o.carries || []).join(', ') || '(nothing)'} — ${o.why || ''}`).join('\n')}\nCut: ${(s.cut || []).map((c) => `${c.id} (${c.reason})`).join('; ') || '(nothing)'}\nWhy: ${s.rationale}`
).join('\n\n')

const shape = await agent(
  `You are the structural editor. You are not a persona — you are the referee, and the only participant allowed to be balanced. You own the shape of this document and you will be the one blamed if it has none.

Settle the SPINE first: one sentence naming what this document is about and what it wants from its reader. Read the five proposed spines, then write the true one. It is usually the thing that forced the document to exist, not a summary of its contents. A spine shaped like "this document covers A, B and C" is a table of contents, not a spine.

Then settle the outline. You are CHOOSING, not accumulating: the union of five thorough outlines is the wall of text you were hired to prevent. Start from the smallest document that serves the spine and would let its reader act, and add only what they would be worse off without.

Binding constraints you must satisfy at once: every load-bearing ledger item either appears in a section or is listed in \`cut\` with a reason (archivist); the reader reaches the point in the first paragraph (skimmer); no section depends on context that is not here (newcomer); no section exists that could belong to another document (hawk); the reader can tell what to do, and what is still unknown (doer). Where they conflict, apply the tie-break rules exactly.

If the ledger holds two or more open questions, the outline MUST include a section that names them. That is not a judgement call.

${REFEREE}

${presetBlock}

${HOUSE}

PROPOSALS:
${proposalBlock}

${COMMON}

Return the settled \`preset\`, \`spine\`, \`outline\` and \`cut\`, with a two-sentence \`rationale\` naming what you chose against and why.`,
  { label: 'editor:shape', phase: 'Shape', schema: SHAPE_SCHEMA }
)

const settled = shape && shape.outline && shape.outline.length ? shape : proposals[0]
const shapeRefereed = settled === shape
// A log line is invisible to the user. Without this flag the run hands back a
// document composed against ONE lopsided persona's outline, with no tie-break
// ever applied, and presents it as refereed.
if (!shapeRefereed) log(`docs-humanize: structural editor failed — fell back to the ${settled.persona || 'first'} proposal, UNREFEREED`)

const shapeBlock = `SETTLED SHAPE — preset ${settled.preset || presetKey || 'bespoke'}
SPINE (fixed; every section serves it): ${settled.spine}
${(settled.outline || []).map((o, i) => `${i + 1}. ${o.heading} [${o.container}] carries ${(o.carries || []).join(', ') || '(nothing)'}`).join('\n')}
DELIBERATELY CUT: ${(settled.cut || []).map((c) => `${c.id} (${c.reason})`).join('; ') || '(nothing)'}
${settled.rationale ? `Editor's reasoning: ${settled.rationale}` : ''}`

// ── Phase 3: one writer, working to a settled shape ─────────────────────────
phase('Compose')
let current = await agent(
  `You are the composing editor. The shape below is settled and is not yours to relitigate; your job is to write the document into it, and to write it the way a thoughtful person would — not the way a model would.

How to write it:
- Lead with the point. The first paragraph says what this is and what it wants from the reader.
- Use the container each section was assigned. If a section is marked table, build the table. If it is marked checklist, every line is independently verifiable. Prose sections are prose — not bullets in disguise.
- Vary your sentence rhythm. Uniform length is the loudest tell that nobody wrote this. Let a short sentence land after a long one.
- Say the concrete thing. "Google's membership API wants a group's internal id, not its email address" beats "memberships are parented on groups/<id>".
- Where the source has a human voice, keep it. You are editing a person's writing, not replacing it: cut rather than rephrase, and let their asides and opinions through. Where the source is generated, write freely — there is no voice to protect.
- State gaps plainly where someone will look for them: "no owner is named", "the date was never decided". Never fill one in.
- Cut every sentence that asserts a benefit instead of a fact.

What you may not do: add a fact, resolve an open question, name an owner the source does not name, invent a link, change a number, or carry a ledger item the editor cut back in. Every sentence traces to a ledger item or to external context named in the purpose digest.

${shapeBlock}

${REFEREE}

${HOUSE}

${COMMON}

Return the finished document as markdown in \`doc\` (no code fence around it), the spine unchanged in \`spine\`, two sentences in \`rationale\` on what you had to cut to make it fit, and anything you could not resolve in \`unresolved\`.`,
  { label: 'compose', phase: 'Compose', schema: DOC_SCHEMA }
)

if (!current || !current.doc) throw new Error('docs-humanize: the composing editor failed; no document to judge.')

// Breaches BLOCK. Everything in here must be fixable by cutting, tightening, or
// reshaping — never by adding words. Being under the band floor is not in this
// list on purpose: the only way an editor could clear it is by writing sentences
// no ledger item supports, which is the one thing this skill exists to prevent.
// It is an observation instead, reported to the human and to nobody's veto.
const report = (m) => {
  const out = []
  if (m.words > Math.round(sourceWords * ceil)) out.push(`length is ${m.words} words (${m.ratio}× the source); the ceiling is ${Math.round(sourceWords * ceil)} — cut ${m.words - Math.round(sourceWords * ceil)}`)
  if (m.longestSentence > LIMITS.maxSentence) out.push(`longest sentence is ${m.longestSentence} words, cap is ${LIMITS.maxSentence}`)
  // Below ten sentences the 90th percentile IS the longest sentence, so this
  // would quietly tighten the real cap from 32 to 24 and call one long sentence
  // in a short ticket "uniformly long".
  if (m.sentenceCount >= 10 && m.p90Sentence > LIMITS.p90Sentence) out.push(`90th-percentile sentence is ${m.p90Sentence} words, cap is ${LIMITS.p90Sentence} — the prose is uniformly long, not just occasionally`)
  if (m.longestParagraph > LIMITS.maxParagraph) out.push(`longest paragraph is ${m.longestParagraph} words, cap is ${LIMITS.maxParagraph}`)
  if (m.maxDepth > LIMITS.maxDepth) out.push(`bullets nest ${m.maxDepth} deep, cap is ${LIMITS.maxDepth}`)
  if (m.emoji > 0) out.push(`${m.emoji} emoji present, cap is 0`)
  if (m.hardSlop.length) out.push(`hard-slop phrases present: ${m.hardSlop.map((h) => `"${h.phrase}"×${h.count}`).join(', ')}`)
  if (m.boldLedeRatio > LIMITS.boldLedeRatio && m.bullets >= 4) out.push(`${Math.round(m.boldLedeRatio * 100)}% of bullets use the "**Noun**:" shape, cap is ${LIMITS.boldLedeRatio * 100}% — it reads as machine output`)
  if (m.thinSections.length) out.push(`heading(s) over a stub of a section: ${m.thinSections.join(', ')} — fold them in or give them content`)
  return out
}

const observe = (m) => {
  const out = []
  if (m.ratio < floor) out.push(`length is ${m.words} words (${m.ratio}× the source), under the band floor of ${Math.round(sourceWords * floor)} — usually right on a padded source, but it is also what over-compression looks like, so check the ledger. Never add words to reach the floor.`)
  if (m.sentenceCount > 0 && m.sentenceCount < 10 && m.p90Sentence > LIMITS.p90Sentence) out.push(`sentences run long (90th percentile ${m.p90Sentence} words) but there are only ${m.sentenceCount} of them, too few to call it a pattern`)
  return out
}

// ── Phase 4: the panel judges real prose ────────────────────────────────────
phase('Judge')
const history = []
let unanimous = false
let round = 0
let lastBlockers = []
let judgesReported = 0
let repaired = false

while (round < ROUNDS) {
  round++
  const m = measure(current.doc)
  const breaches = report(m)
  const observations = observe(m)
  const verdicts = (await parallel(PERSONAS.map((p) => () =>
    agent(
      `You are ${p.title}, judging round ${round} of a rewrite.

${p.charter}

YOUR VETO CONDITIONS — you may block on nothing else:
${p.veto}

The other judges block on their own conditions and the editor must satisfy all of them at once. Any fix you propose must not violate theirs:
${vetoTable}

${REFEREE}

${HOUSE}

SPINE the document is built on: ${current.spine || settled.spine}

CANDIDATE DOCUMENT:
${current.doc}

MEASURED AGAINST ITS LIMITS (computed, not estimated):
${breaches.length ? breaches.map((b) => `- BREACH: ${b}`).join('\n') : '- within every measured limit'}
${observations.length ? observations.map((o) => `- OBSERVATION (not a breach, and not grounds for anyone's veto — nobody may ask for words to be added): ${o}`).join('\n') : ''}
${m.softSlop.length ? `- soft-slop words present (evidence, not a breach — judge them in context): ${m.softSlop.map((h) => `"${h.phrase}"×${h.count}`).join(', ')}` : ''}

${COMMON}

Set \`verdict\` to "accept" if nothing in your veto list is triggered — accepting a document you merely would have written differently is the correct outcome, and the most common mistake here is blocking on taste. Otherwise "revise", with each blocking objection carrying an exact \`quote\`, a one-sentence \`problem\`, and a minimal \`fix\`. Use \`note\` for non-blocking preferences; they will be ignored, which is the point.`,
      { label: `judge:r${round}:${p.key}`, phase: 'Judge', schema: JUDGE_SCHEMA }
    ).then((v) => (v ? { persona: p.key, ...v } : null))
  ))).filter(Boolean)

  // A dead judge must never read as a silent accept, or the run reports a
  // consensus nobody voted in.
  judgesReported = verdicts.length
  const blockers = verdicts
    .filter((v) => v.verdict === 'revise')
    .flatMap((v) => (v.blocking || []).map((b) => ({ ...b, from: v.persona })))

  history.push({
    round,
    voted: verdicts.length,
    verdicts: verdicts.map((v) => ({ persona: v.persona, verdict: v.verdict, blocking: (v.blocking || []).length })),
    blockers: blockers.map((b) => ({ persona: b.from, quote: b.quote, problem: b.problem })),
    breaches,
    observations,
  })
  lastBlockers = blockers

  if (!blockers.length && !breaches.length) {
    if (verdicts.length === PERSONAS.length) {
      unanimous = true
      log(`docs-humanize: unanimous accept in round ${round}`)
    } else {
      log(`docs-humanize: round ${round} — no blocking objections, but only ${verdicts.length}/${PERSONAS.length} judges reported; not calling this unanimous`)
    }
    break
  }

  log(`docs-humanize: round ${round} — ${blockers.length} blocking objection(s)${blockers.length ? ` from ${[...new Set(blockers.map((b) => b.from))].join(', ')}` : ''}, ${breaches.length} measured breach(es)`)

  const finalRound = round >= ROUNDS
  if (finalRound && ROUNDS > 1) break

  const revised = await agent(
    `You are the composing editor. Round ${round} produced these BLOCKING objections and measured breaches. Apply the minimal set of edits that clears every one without triggering a different persona's veto.

${blockers.length ? `OBJECTIONS:\n${blockers.map((b) => `- [${b.from}] "${b.quote}" — ${b.problem} → ${b.fix}`).join('\n')}` : '(no persona objections)'}

${breaches.length ? `MEASURED BREACHES — each is a defect, not an opinion:\n${breaches.map((b) => `- ${b}`).join('\n')}` : '(within every measured limit)'}

Rewrite minimally. Do not restructure sections nobody objected to, and do not "improve" while you are in there — churn loses the parts the panel accepted. The spine and the settled shape stand unless an objection is specifically about them. If two objections are genuinely irreconcilable, apply the tie-break rules and record the losing one in \`unresolved\` with one line on why it lost.

Above all: you may not clear a length breach by dropping a load-bearing fact. Cut filler, merge sections, shorten sentences. If the facts genuinely do not fit, say so in \`unresolved\` and leave them in. And no breach above is ever cleared by ADDING words — a document shorter than expected is not a defect, and padding it would mean writing sentences no ledger item supports.

${shapeBlock}

${REFEREE}

${HOUSE}

CURRENT DOCUMENT:
${current.doc}

${COMMON}`,
    { label: `editor:r${round}`, phase: 'Judge', schema: DOC_SCHEMA }
  )

  if (revised && revised.doc) {
    current = { ...revised, spine: revised.spine || current.spine || settled.spine, unresolved: [...(current.unresolved || []), ...(revised.unresolved || [])] }
    if (finalRound) repaired = true
  } else {
    log(`docs-humanize: editor failed in round ${round}; keeping the previous candidate`)
    break
  }
}

// ── Phase 5: did it stay true? ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Adversarial rewriting drifts, and it drifts toward fluency. Three rounds of
// "make it plainer" is exactly how a hedged accurate sentence becomes a clean,
// confident, wrong one — and nobody downstream will have the original to check
// it against, which is why this phase exists at all.
phase('Audit')
const coverage = await agent(
  `You are the fidelity auditor. You are not a stylist. You have no opinion about length, tone, or structure, and you must not raise one.

Three jobs, in this order:

1. MISSING. For every LOAD-BEARING ledger item, decide whether its content is present in the rewritten document — present in substance, not word for word; a fact said more briefly is present. List in \`missing\` every load-bearing item that is simply gone, with its id and why its loss matters. Items the structural editor deliberately cut are listed below; a cut item is still missing if its loss changes what a reader believes or does, and you should say so.

2. INVENTED. For every claim in the rewritten document, find the ledger item or the external-context statement in the purpose digest that it traces to. List in \`invented\` any claim that traces to neither — a rationale nobody gave, an owner nobody named, a number nobody wrote, a next step nobody agreed to, a link that does not appear in the source. This is the most important job on this run. Invented sentences read exactly like real ones, usually better, and nobody downstream has the original to check them against.

3. DISTORTED. List in \`distorted\` any item whose meaning changed: a hedge turned into a certainty, an open question quietly answered, a conflict silently resolved, "some" become "all", a number rounded, an identifier paraphrased.

Set \`verdict\` to "repair" if any list is non-empty, "faithful" otherwise. Each entry needs a quote and a correction that preserves the document's plain language. An empty result is the expected outcome of a good run — do not invent findings to look thorough.

DELIBERATELY CUT BY THE EDITOR, with the editor's reason: ${(settled.cut || []).map((c) => `${c.id} (${c.reason})`).join('; ') || '(nothing)'}

REWRITTEN DOCUMENT:
${current.doc}

${COMMON}`,
  { label: 'audit:fidelity', phase: 'Audit', schema: COVERAGE_SCHEMA }
)

// A dead auditor must not present as a clean bill of health — that turns the
// phase guarding against invention into a source of false assurance.
const auditOk = Boolean(coverage)
const missing = (coverage && coverage.missing) || []
const invented = (coverage && coverage.invented) || []
const distorted = (coverage && coverage.distorted) || []
if (!auditOk) log('docs-humanize: the fidelity auditor failed — this rewrite was NOT verified against the ledger')

if (missing.length || invented.length || distorted.length) {
  log(`docs-humanize: auditor flagged ${missing.length} missing, ${invented.length} invented, ${distorted.length} distorted; repairing`)
  const fixed = await agent(
    `You are the composing editor. Fix ONLY what is listed below, in place. Do not restyle anything else and do not undo the structure — it is what makes this readable.

${invented.length ? `INVENTED — these trace to nothing in the source. Cut each, or replace it with the plain statement of what is actually unknown:\n${invented.map((e) => `- "${e.quote}" — ${e.problem} → ${e.correction}`).join('\n')}\n` : ''}
${distorted.length ? `DISTORTED — restore the original meaning, including its hedge:\n${distorted.map((e) => `- "${e.quote}" — ${e.problem} → ${e.correction}`).join('\n')}\n` : ''}
${missing.length ? `MISSING — restore each in the shortest form that carries the fact:\n${missing.map((e) => `- ${e.id}: ${e.text} (matters because: ${e.whyItMatters})`).join('\n')}\nYou are still bound by the limits, so pay for each restored fact by cutting something that carries less. Do not simply append a section at the end.\n` : ''}

${HOUSE}

CURRENT DOCUMENT:
${current.doc}

${COMMON}`,
    { label: 'editor:repair', phase: 'Audit', schema: DOC_SCHEMA }
  )
  if (fixed && fixed.doc) current = { ...current, doc: fixed.doc }
}

const final = measure(current.doc)
const finalBreaches = report(final)
const finalObservations = observe(final)
log(`docs-humanize: ${provenance}/${density} source of ${sourceWords} words → ${final.words} words (${final.ratio}×), ${round} round(s), unanimous=${unanimous}, ${judgesReported}/${PERSONAS.length} judges reported, ${auditOk ? `${missing.length} restored / ${invented.length} invented claim(s) cut / ${distorted.length} distortion(s) fixed` : 'AUDIT DID NOT COMPLETE'}${finalBreaches.length ? `, ${finalBreaches.length} LIMIT BREACH(ES) REMAIN` : ', within every limit'}${shapeRefereed ? '' : ', SHAPE UNREFEREED'}${finalObservations.length ? `, ${finalObservations.length} observation(s)` : ''}`)

return {
  doc: current.doc,
  spine: current.spine || settled.spine,
  preset: settled.preset || presetKey || 'bespoke',
  outline: (settled.outline || []).map((o) => o.heading),
  cut: settled.cut || [],
  provenance,
  density,
  worstFailure,
  sourceWords,
  words: final.words,
  ratio: final.ratio,
  band: [Math.round(sourceWords * floor), Math.round(sourceWords * ceil)],
  ledgerSize: items.length,
  loadBearing: bearing.length,
  openQuestions: openQuestions.map((q) => q.text),
  unanimous,
  repaired,
  judgesReported,
  rounds: round,
  history,
  outstanding: unanimous ? [] : lastBlockers.map((b) => `[${b.from}] ${b.problem}`),
  unresolved: [...(current.unresolved || []), ...(shapeRefereed ? [] : [`the structural editor failed; the shape is one persona's (${settled.persona || 'unknown'}) with no tie-breaks applied`])],
  audited: auditOk,
  missing,
  invented,
  distorted,
  breaches: finalBreaches,
  observations: finalObservations,
  shapeRefereed,
  softSlop: final.softSlop,
}
````

## Step 2b: Surface the Run ID for `wfwatch`

The **Workflow** tool runs the swarm in the background and its tool result includes a **run ID** (`wf_…`). The user has a shell function, **`wfwatch`**, that live-tails a run by that ID.

**In the same turn that launches the workflow — before you yield — print the run ID with the exact command:**

```
✍️  docs-humanize launched on "<title>" — run ID: wf_ab12cd34
    Watch it live in another terminal:  wfwatch wf_ab12cd34
    One-shot snapshot instead:          wfwatch wf_ab12cd34 --once
```

Use the **actual** `runId` from the Workflow tool result verbatim.

**Then end your turn — never block the session on the swarm.** The run continues in the background and a task notification re-invokes you when it finishes. No `TaskOutput` on the workflow's task, no `Monitor`, no `ScheduleWakeup`, no polling the journal, no sleep, no filler tool calls. None of it makes the result arrive sooner and all of it costs the user their session. If they send other prompts meanwhile, answer them normally. The completion notification is the only thing that resumes this skill; when it arrives, continue with Step 3.

## Step 3: Present the Result

**Output `doc` inside a single markdown code block** (triple backticks, no language tag) so the user can copy raw markdown. Nothing else goes inside that block.

Above it, print the **spine** on its own line. It is the fastest way to catch a run that went wrong: if the spine is not what the document is about, nothing below it will be either, and re-running beats editing.

Under the code block, print at most six lines of run record, and only what is true:

```
spine: the migration is blocked on one undecided retention number, and nobody owns deciding it
generated/padded source · 1,840 → 610 words (0.33×, band 460–920) · design preset · 2 rounds · unanimous · 5 of 5 judges
  r1 · doer blocked "the team will coordinate rollout" · hawk blocked "seamless"
  audit · 2 invented claims cut · 1 hedge restored · 0 facts missing
  open questions carried through: 3
  cut from the source: F7 (restated intro), F12 (benefit with no fact)
```

Rules for that record: it is a log, not a defense. Do not justify the text and do not offer to lengthen it. **Every line must come from a field the workflow returned** — never reconstruct how an objection was resolved, which the workflow does not report; inferring it from the final text fabricates a provenance log, the exact failure the audit phase exists to prevent.

Then, specifically:

- **`audited` false** → print `fidelity audit did not complete — this rewrite is unverified against the source` on its own line and report no counts. This is the one result that should stop a write-back: say plainly that you would not paste it anywhere yet.
- **`invented` non-empty** → say how many claims traced to nothing and were cut. This is the most useful number in the run, and a repeat offender means the purpose digest was thin.
- **`missing` non-empty** → name the facts the rewrite lost and the auditor restored.
- **`unanimous` false** → say so and list `outstanding`.
- **`repaired` true** → note that the last round's objections were applied but never re-judged.
- **`judgesReported` under 5** → print `N of 5 judges reported` and never print "unanimous".
- **`breaches` non-empty** → print them verbatim. The document is outside its measured limits and the honest move is to offer another pass or to say the source may be two documents. When `unanimous` is false and `outstanding` is empty, the breaches are the reason — say so, rather than printing a non-unanimous verdict with nothing behind it.
- **`observations` non-empty** → print them as observations, never as defects. Nothing in that list is a thing to fix: the usual entry is a document that came in under the band floor, which on a padded source is the skill working. It is there so a human can glance at the ledger, and the one response it must never produce is adding words.
- **`shapeRefereed` false** → say plainly that the structural editor died and the document was built on one persona's outline with no tie-breaks applied. That is the result most worth re-running, because a lopsided shape is invisible in a well-written document.
- **`openQuestions` non-empty** → list them under the record even though they are in the document. They are what the reader most needs to see, and they are what a generated source most reliably hides.
- **`ratio` below the band floor** → it arrives in `observations`; see above. Never offer to lengthen the document to reach the floor.

If the workflow returns no usable `doc`, say the run failed and do not hand-write a replacement — a hand rewrite with no ledger behind it is exactly the thing this skill exists to prevent. Offer to re-run instead.

## Step 4: The Write-Back Gate

**Nothing is written anywhere until the user says yes in chat.** Under `--no-write`, skip this step entirely.

First, **save the original.** Write the fetched source verbatim to the session scratchpad and name the path in chat. Linear and Notion keep their own history and a tracked file has git, but an untracked markdown file has nothing, and you are about to replace it.

Then offer, naming the exact target:

- **Notion** — two options, and say which you recommend. `notion-create-suggested-edit` leaves the author a reviewable diff and is the right default when `sourceMeta.isOwn` is false or the page has other collaborators. `notion-update-page` overwrites the body and is right for the user's own draft. Never pick overwrite silently on a page the user does not own.
- **Linear** — `save_issue` with the new `description`. The title is **not** rewritten unless the user asks: a renamed ticket breaks every link anyone has pasted into Slack. For a set, write each ticket separately and report each result; one failure does not abort the rest, and you say which ones landed.
- **File** — `Write` the file. If it is untracked, say so when you ask: there is no undo but the scratchpad copy.

Ask once, for all targets, and take a single yes. After writing, confirm what changed where, with URLs.

## Step 5: Sets — the Consistency Pass

After all the runs in a set finish, before offering any write-back:

1. **Terminology** — check every document against the glossary. One noun, one gloss, everywhere. A term that drifted is a defect in the documents that drifted, not a reason to update the glossary.
2. **Shape** — tickets in a set should share a preset. One ticket with acceptance criteria and three without is worse than all four lacking them, because the reader concludes the three are unscoped.
3. **Overlap and gaps** — two tickets claiming the same work, or a dependency named in one and invisible in the others, is a finding about the *plan* and not about the writing. Surface it; do not fix it silently.
4. **Open questions** — collect them across the whole set into one list and print it. A question appearing in three tickets is usually one decision nobody has made, and that is the single most valuable thing this skill produces on a set.

## Important Rules

1. **The packet is the run.** Subagents see only `args`. A thin `purpose` digest produces a fluent rewrite of the wrong document, and it will read well — which is what makes it dangerous.
2. **Never invent.** If the source does not say why, the rewrite does not say why. "No owner is named" is a finished sentence.
3. **Uncertainty is content.** The open questions are the most valuable thing in most generated documents, because they are what the generator smoothed over. They survive every pass.
4. **The original is sacred until the user says otherwise.** Save it before any write, offer suggested edits over overwrites on anything the user does not own, and never rename a ticket.
5. **Format is not up for debate.** The house rules bind every persona, including the thorough one who wants a background section.
6. **Match the tool to the document.** A four-line ticket does not need a 23-agent swarm. For something trivial, say so and offer to tighten it directly, or run `--quick`.
7. **Report honestly.** A non-unanimous result, a failed audit, or a residual breach is a real signal about the document — usually that it is two documents — not a formatting problem to smooth over.
8. **The limits are arithmetic, not taste.** They are measured in code and handed to the agents precisely because "make it concise" is satisfied by six 40-word sentences.
9. **Shape is settled before prose.** The panel decides what the document is and what order it goes in; the composing editor decides how it reads and may not change what it claims. An editor allowed to do both trades facts for flow and calls it tightening.
10. **A human voice outranks polish.** Where someone actually wrote something, this skill edits. It only writes free-hand over prose that a machine produced.

## Step 6: Self-Improvement

After the run, reflect:

- Did the auditor catch invented claims? Each one traces to either a thin `purpose` digest or a persona charter that licensed invention. Fix whichever it was.
- Did the same objection ping-pong between skimmer and newcomer? The tie-break rules need sharpening — that is a script edit, not a prompt tweak.
- Did a persona block on something outside its veto list? Tighten that persona's veto wording.
- Did the measured band fight the document — a dense human source compressed into mush, or a padded one let off easy? The bands are a table in the script; move the number rather than working around it.
- Did the user still cut the result by hand? Whatever they cut is a missing veto condition. Add it.
- Did a hard-slop pattern fire on a legitimate use? Move it to the soft list. One false positive teaches the whole panel to ignore the measure.

If anything would have gone better with different instructions, **edit this skill file** (`~/.claude/skills/docs-humanize/SKILL.md`) surgically, and say briefly what changed and why.
